# Element Attributes

Element **attributes** give additional information about an element. For example, an attribute for an `<a>` (link) element might specify _where_ the link goes. An attributes for an `<img>` (image) element might specify _which image_ to display. Attributes have a _name_ and a _value_, specified in the opening tag of the element. For example, the following code creates a link to Google:

```{html autorun}
<a href="https://www.google.com">Google</a>
```

In this code, `href` is the name of the attribute and `https://www.google.com` is the value of the attribute. The value is enclosed in double quotes (`"`). The `href` attribute specifies the URL that the link goes to when clicked.

### Multiple Attributes

Elements can also have multiple attributes. For example, the following code creates an image with a `src` (source) attribute that specifies the URL of the image and an `alt` (alternative text) attribute that specifies the text to display if the image cannot be loaded:

```{html autorun}
<img src="images/puppy.jpg" alt="A puppy sitting in the grass" />
```

![A diagram showing the attributes of an HTML element](images/attributes.png)

## Boolean Attributes (Attributes Without Values)

In some cases, the meaning of an attribute is determined by its presence or absence. For example, the `disabled` attribute for `<button>` elements specifies that the button is disabled and cannot be clicked. We could set the value to be anything; `disabled="true"`, `disabled="disabled"`, `disabled="false"`, or even `disabled=""` (an empty string) would all make the `<button />` disabled.

In these cases, we can omit the value and just write the attribute name. For example, the following code creates a disabled button:

```{html autorun}
<button disabled>You cannot click me</button>
<button>You can click me</button>
```

## Exercises

1. Create a link (`<a>`) element that links to `https://www.umich.edu/` and has the text "University of Michigan". The link should open in a new tab when clicked. You can do this with the following attributes:
   - `href`: This specifies the URL the link goes to. Set this to `https://www.umich.edu/`.
   - `target`: This specifies the tab where the link should open. Set this to `_blank` to open the link in a new tab.

   ```{html}

   ```

   ```+solution
   <a href="https://www.umich.edu/" target="_blank">University of Michigan</a>
   ```

   ```+test
   assert('a').should('exist').run('Could not find an a element', 'Created an a element')
   assert('a').should('have.attr', 'href', 'https://www.umich.edu/').run('The a element does not have the href attribute set to https://www.umich.edu/', 'Created an a element with the href attribute set to https://www.umich.edu/')
   assert('a').should('have.attr', 'target', '_blank').run('The a element does not have the target attribute set to _blank', 'Created an a element with the target attribute set to _blank')
   assert('a').should('have.text', 'University of Michigan').run('The a element does not have the text "University of Michigan"', 'Created an a element with the text "University of Michigan"')
   ```

2. Create an `<input>` element with the following attributes:
   - `type` set to `"text"` (this specifies that the input is a text box)
   - `placeholder` set to `"Enter your name"` (this specifies the text that will be displayed in the input box when it is empty).


   ```{html}

   ```

   ```+solution
   <input type="text" placeholder="Enter your name" />
   ```

   ```+test
   assert('input').should('exist').run('Could not find an input element', 'Created an input element')
   assert('input').should('have.attr', 'type', 'text').run('The input element does not have the type attribute set to text', 'Created an input element with the type attribute set to text')
   assert('input').should('have.attr', 'placeholder', 'Enter your name').run('The input element does not have the placeholder attribute set to Enter your name', 'Created an input element with the placeholder attribute set to Enter your name')
   ```

3. Create an `<img>` element with the following attributes:
   - `src` set to `images/cat.jpg` (this specifies the URL of the image to display)
   - `alt` set to "A cat" (this specifies the text to display if the image cannot be loaded)
   - `width` set to `200px` (this specifies the width of the image)

   ```{html}

   ```
   ```+solution
   <img src="images/cat.jpg" alt="A cat" width="200px" />
   ```
   ```+test
   assert('img').should('exist').run('Could not find an img element', 'Created an img element')
   assert('img').should('have.attribute', 'src', 'images/cat.jpg').run('The img element does not have the src attribute set to images/cat.jpg', 'Created an img element with the src attribute set to images/cat.jpg')
   assert('img').should('have.attribute', 'alt', 'A cat').run('The img element does not have the alt attribute set to A cat', 'Created an img element with the alt attribute set to A cat')
   assert('img').should('have.attribute', 'width', '200px').run('The img element does not have the width attribute set to 200px', 'Created an img element with the width attribute set to 200px')
   ```

